@startuml 04-concurrent-booking
skinparam backgroundColor White
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Sans
skinparam roundCorner 6
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam ArrowColor #333333
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFFFF0
skinparam NoteBorderColor #999999
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F4F8
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #555555
skinparam ClassBackgroundColor #F4F4F8
skinparam ClassBorderColor #555555
skinparam StateBackgroundColor #F4F4F8
skinparam StateBorderColor #555555
skinparam ComponentBackgroundColor #F4F4F8
skinparam ComponentBorderColor #555555
title No over-booking\nTwo clients race for the same seat, and only one can win

participant "Client A" as A
participant "Client B" as B
box "moviebackend process" #F8F8F8
    participant "worker thread A" as WA
    participant "worker thread B" as WB
    participant "Catalog\n(one std::mutex)" as C
    participant "SeatMap for s1" as S
end box

note over A, S
  Both clients were shown seat **a1** as free, because both
  fetched the seat map before either of them booked. That is
  normal and unavoidable: the displayed availability is a
  snapshot, not a reservation.
end note

A -> WA : POST /showtimes/s1/bookings {"seats":["a1"]}
B -> WB : POST /showtimes/s1/bookings {"seats":["a1"]}

par the two requests arrive at the same instant
    WA -> C : bookSeats("s1", {"a1"}, "Alice")
else
    WB -> C : bookSeats("s1", {"a1"}, "Bob")
end

note over C #FFE8E8
  Both threads now call the same method
  at the same time. The mutex is what
  decides the order; whichever thread
  acquires it first wins.
end note

== Thread A acquires the lock ==
activate C #LightGreen
WA -> C : lock_guard(m_mutex) acquired
C -> S : isFree("a1")?
S --> C : **yes**
C -> S : book("a1")
S --> C : a1 is now Booked
C -> C : create Booking b1

note left of WB #FFF8E0
  Thread B is **blocked** here, inside
  lock_guard's constructor. It has not
  looked at the seat yet, so it cannot
  have seen the stale "free" value.
end note

C -> C : unlock
deactivate C
C --> WA : Booking b1
WA -> A : **201 Created**\n{"id":"b1","seats":["a1"]}

== Thread B now acquires the lock ==
activate C #Pink
WB -> C : lock_guard(m_mutex) acquired
C -> S : isFree("a1")?
S --> C : **no - already Booked**
C -->x WB : throw ServiceError(SeatUnavailable)
deactivate C

WB -> WB : the exception reaches RestErrorHandler
WB -> B : **409 Conflict**\n{"code":"SEAT_UNAVAILABLE",\n "message":"seat 'a1' is already booked"}

note over A, S #E8FFE8
  **The invariant, in one line:**
  successful bookings == seats taken == seats no longer available.

  Because the check and the act happen inside one critical section,
  there is no window in which both threads can observe "free".
  With 16 threads instead of 2, exactly one still wins - that is
  what CatalogConcurrencyTest and the Python smoke tests assert.
end note

note over C #F0F0FF
  **Multi-seat requests are all-or-nothing.**
  A request for {a1, a2} where a2 is taken books
  *neither* seat: every seat is validated in a first
  pass, and only then does the second pass mutate,
  so the commit phase cannot fail halfway.
end note

@enduml
